    id: "r_bees", title: "Bees and the Food on Our Plates", min: 16, lim: "NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS",
    paras: [
      "About one third of the food that humans eat depends, at least partly, on pollination by insects, and bees are by far the most important of these insects. When a bee visits a flower to collect nectar, grains of pollen stick to its body and are carried to the next flower, allowing the plant to produce fruit and seeds. Apples, almonds, coffee, tomatoes and many other crops would give much smaller harvests without this help.",
      "Since around 2006, beekeepers in North America and Europe have reported alarming losses. In some winters, more than a third of all managed honeybee colonies in the United States died. The phenomenon was given a name, colony collapse disorder, because in many hives the worker bees simply disappeared, leaving the queen, the young bees and plenty of honey behind.",
      "Scientists do not believe that a single cause is responsible. Instead, they point to a combination of factors. A parasite called the varroa mite feeds on bees and spreads viruses between them. A group of pesticides known as neonicotinoids, which are widely used on crops such as oilseed rape, appear to damage the ability of bees to find their way back to the hive. In addition, large farms that grow only one crop offer bees a poor and limited diet.",
      "Wild bees may be in even greater danger than honeybees, because nobody looks after them. There are around 20,000 species of bee in the world, and many of them nest in the ground or in old wood. When hedges are removed and fields are ploughed right up to the edge, these nesting places disappear. A study in Britain found that wild bees had disappeared from a quarter of the places where they had been recorded in 1980.",
      "There are, however, reasons for hope. In 2018, the European Union banned the outdoor use of three neonicotinoid pesticides. Farmers in several countries are now paid to leave strips of wild flowers at the edges of their fields, and city councils are planting flowers in parks instead of cutting the grass short. Even ordinary people can help: a small garden with a variety of flowers that bloom at different times of year can feed bees from spring to autumn.",
    ],
